# Prose-budget ratchet baseline — issue #589.
#
# Files currently OVER their type budget in
# check-decomposition/thresholds.yml (bloat_thresholds). The gate fails a
# file above max(its type budget, its entry here), so these are frozen at
# their present size: they may shrink freely, and any growth fails.
#
# Regenerate with: tests/lint-prose-budget.sh --regen
# RAISING an entry is a deliberate, reviewable decision — a bigger file
# needs a reason in the commit message, not a silent regen.
#
# THE BUDGETS ARE TARGETS, NOT LAWS. Some files are genuinely better long.
# This file is the EXCEPTIONS LEDGER: each entry is consciously over
# budget, the ratchet stops it growing, and the trailing `#` note says WHY
# it is allowed. Add a rationale when you add an entry — --regen preserves
# existing ones, and the report lists any entry still missing one.
#
# Format: <repo-relative-path> <line-count> [# why this exception stands]
#
# NOTE: --regen rewrites this header from the script and preserves only the
# PER-ENTRY `# why` notes. Free-form header prose like the block below is not
# carried across a regen — re-add it, or move it into the entries themselves.
#
# Prose-decomposition slices — what was deliberately NOT done, so it is not
# re-derived:
#   * checker.md is DONE (#717) and is no longer a ledger entry. #503 moved
#     Step 3a; #717 moved Step 2's discovery gates, Step 6's agnix precedence,
#     and Step 7's direct-dispatch trail to skill companions, landing it at 391
#     (under the 400 agent bar). Each cut was justified by LOAD FREQUENCY or
#     CALL-SPECIFICITY, never by coupling — the pre-scan still reports the 7
#     steps as "mutually referential", which is expected and not a blocker.
#   * audit-ai-config.md (273) is 23 over its 250 warn with 11-22 line sections;
#     extraction would cost more indirection than it saves. Justified in place,
#     which #503 AC2 explicitly permits ("or an explicit justification why not").
#     It is under its 400 high bar, so it is not a ledger entry.
#   * No workflow.js can be split into importable siblings (#90, #91, re-probed
#     live in #807). #718 settled the per-harness decision (#503 AC3); #808 then
#     landed the #806 generator, so the recorded answers now split by whether a
#     harness is ENROLLED in it:
#       - ship-issue, codebase-audit: STRUCTURABLE, applied via the workflow.src/
#         fragment layout. Their decisions live in the fragments (05-provenance.js
#         and 10-config.js), never in the generated artifact.
#       - orchestrate: STRUCTURABLE, already applied — the in-file entry-point +
#         banner shape, and the reference for anything not enrolled.
#       - code-reviewer: STRUCTURABLE, applied in-file (runReview() + a column-0
#         dispatch tail, pinned by name in its test area).
#     #718 also closed the other two levers by measurement, and the generator does
#     NOT reopen either: prompt-string share is flat over time (ship-issue was
#     24%/44% at 649 raw lines and 24%/44% at 1930), so there is no accreted prose
#     to trim; and the 382 cross-harness duplicated lines stay the BUDGET_FLOOR
#     class, because the generated artifact still cannot import — fragments are
#     shareable within a harness, never across them.
plugins/workflow/skills/orchestrate/mode-protocol.md 877 # carries all orchestration modes; splitting per-mode would scatter one decision table an operator reads end-to-end
plugins/workflow/skills/ship-issue/ci-review-protocol.md 755 # the CI-wait + multi-cycle review contract; its length is the protocol, and every ship path loads it whole. +6 in #815: <skill-base-dir> substitution notes on the four recipes here that run worktree-isolated, plus the reader-vs-writer sentence on the golem-notify pipe (an exemption asserted in an earlier draft and measured FALSE in review — the note is what stops it being re-asserted)
